[{"id":"VRCE-2-6-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":6,"verse":1,"english":"When the priest had gone away, Rāma, having bathed and with a collected mind, began to adore Nārāyaṇa, in company with his wife having expansive eyes.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2006001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f5e8d98fb7a7666071f48402fef65eba1b7029abab4798cd510a93290ddd1213","compact":"a91c55e968c598eaa157bd5e5e6503f54e10f155537983f9b9f9e2b3b0af7941","nasal":"a91c55e968c598eaa157bd5e5e6503f54e10f155537983f9b9f9e2b3b0af7941"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-6-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":6,"verse":2,"english":"Then raising the vessel of clarified butter to his head (by way of paying reverence), he in accordance with the ordinance began to offer oblations to the flaming fire on behalf of that mighty deity.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.638,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2006002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"484ebf963e4a68bb0195f559f1efec8c736ddd98e5368f6320f91f2e2781fb65","compact":"a68f79a834ad8eeadf6d71b2d97baf9a06541d4bc69f6645f48cdba5fb4d639b","nasal":"a68f79a834ad8eeadf6d71b2d97baf9a06541d4bc69f6645f48cdba5fb4d639b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-6-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":6,"verse":3,"english":"Then, having partaken of the remaining quantity of the clarified butter, Rāma prayed for his own welfare, and meditated on the god Nārāyaṇa. The son of the best of men with a collected mind, and restraining his speech lay down on a Kuśa bed together with Vaidehī within the graceful dwelling of Vişnu.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.892,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"sour