erances of genealogists and panegyrist and Brāhmaṇas versed in the Purāņas, Rāma finished devotions for the prior twilight, and with an intent mind began to recite (Savatri).* *The celebrated hymn to the sun, the most sacred thing in all the Vedas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.75,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2006006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"79842e74ac84c43f876c8652b71c23f2759efb2c0952ffa52178d2dd57c6cdc9","compact":"4e6e3996e01cd003398628eb66a3854292979f1c337e5a2b372b3fd99e6a537f","nasal":"9d8bf4043fb26bf000f43b1a0a40885c25793d4173ffb572b83a5e1f6bba0fda"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-6-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":6,"verse":7,"english":"And clad in a clear silk dress, he with bended head hymned the destroyer of Madhu, and made the regenerate ones perform the Svastivácana ceremony.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.714,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2006007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f10404401bfcbe0bd3158856f791571667e40d91a020e08e88870ed702fcdbdc","compact":"e9bb84743dffc714290a1200bff3dbaaa4738b45f405f96553c93924bef62360","nasal":"e9bb84743dffc714290a1200bff3dbaaa4738b45f405f96553c93924bef62360"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-6-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":6,"verse":8,"english":"Already resounding with the blares of trumpets, Ayodhyā became filled with the sweet and solemn tones of the expressions “Holy day\" uttered by them.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-6-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2006008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0c1cacff6a27befafc3599e16cafcffabbff16774a7a77399a747eea5